<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.8.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01393</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.8.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>71b532cd-2051-4ce0-aa75-308c8363e24c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="f224515e-e330-469d-bed0-b67356dd5cf7" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
  <!--Q1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="54e1ee56-0684-4f78-9d2e-b434955a8f28" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\( f(t)=20(\frac{1}{3})^t\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\( f(t)=20+ \\frac{1}{3}t\\)&quot;, &quot;\\( f(t)=20(\\frac{1}{3})^t\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(t)=20(\\frac{2}{3})^t\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(t)=20+\\frac{2}{3}t\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>For an experiment, a scientist designs a can, 20 cm in height, that holds water. A tube is installed at the
          bottom of the can allowing water to drain out.
          <para>At the beginning of the experiment, the can is full. Every minute after the start of the experiment,
            \(\frac23\) of the water is drained.</para>
          <para>The height of the water, \(h\), in cm, is a function \(f\) of time \(t\) in minutes since the
            beginning of the experiment, \(h=f(t)\). Which is an expression for \(f(t)\)?</para>
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.8.2 – 5.8.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="cdc0de8f-f7ad-4b24-a42e-b6c7217de1fa" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\( b=10000 \cdot 3^d\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\( b=10000 \\cdot 3^d\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(b=10000 \\cdot 3d\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(b=10000(\\frac13)^d\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(b=10000+3^d\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>A bacteria population is 10,000. It triples each day. The bacteria population, \(b\), is a function of
          the number of days, \(d\). Which equation relates \(b\) and \(d\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.8.2 – 5.8.4 to test your understanding? Then come
        back and try this question again.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="0b3534d1-9dcd-4d60-b28a-7553f9e04c3c" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\( a=20 \cdot 1.1^t\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(a=20+1.1^t\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(a=20 \\cdot 1.1t\\)&quot;, &quot;\\( a=20 \\cdot 1.1^t\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(a=20-1.1^t\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The area, \(a\), covered by a city is 20 square miles. The area grows by a factor of 1.1 each year, \(t\),
          since it was 20 square miles. Which equation expresses \(a\) in terms of \(t\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.8.2 – 5.8.4. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="5778ce9d-a0fd-4fb0-ab3a-3b9bae8a95dc" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="After 2 minutes, the height of the water is \(\frac{20}{9}\) centimeters." data-solution-options="[&quot;After 2 minutes, the height of the water is \\(\\frac{20}{9}\\) centimeters less than at the start.&quot;,&quot;After \\(\\frac{20}{9}\\) minutes, the height of the water is 2 centimeters.&quot;,&quot;After 2 minutes, the height of the water is \\(\\frac{20}{9}\\) centimeters.&quot;,&quot;After \\(\\frac{20}{9}\\) minutes, the height of the water is 2 centimeters less than at the start.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The graph below models the water draining out of a tube during an experiment represented by the function, \(f(t) = 20(1/3)^t\).
          What does the value \((2, \frac{20}9\)) mean in the context of the problem? <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/58c7b5ead8561c6b71a8fcb3563b7d584d51cf0b"/></media></figure></para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.8.4.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="55059bdc-fd74-48e4-855f-94fcbe04bbe3" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="1" data-solution="Yes" data-solution-options="[&quot;Yes&quot;, &quot;No&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> A scientist measures the height, \(h\), of a tree each month, and \(m\) is the number of months since the
          scientist first measured the height of the tree. Is the height, \(h\), a function of the month, \(m\)?
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.8.3. </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q6-->
  <exercise data-content-id="720112a8-813a-4511-bc7c-4bf5a1f76b02" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(P=3 \cdot 2^m\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\( P=3+2m\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(P=3(2m)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(P=3 \\cdot 2^m\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(P=2 \\cdot 3^m\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Cary started with 3 goldfish in her tank, and the population doubled every month. Which is an equation that
          represents the population, \(P\), of goldfish after \(m\) months?
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.8.2 – 5.8.4 to test your understanding? Then come
        back and try this question again.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q#7-->
  <exercise data-content-id="1c91e30d-674a-4571-92ed-4e12263bbee2" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="50">
    <problem>
      <para>Use this equation for problems 7 – 10:
        \(f(t)=50 \cdot 2^t\)</para>
      <para>What is the initial value?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The initial value is 50. When
        \(t=0\), \(f(t)=50 \cdot 2^0=f(t)=(50)(1)=50\).</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.8.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q#8-->
  <exercise data-content-id="e40d2def-b7bf-4139-8f97-ed505f105823" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="2">
    <problem>
      <para>What is the growth factor?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para> Correct! The initial value is 2. The growth factor refers to the constant multiplier that determines how the
        function grows or decays as the independent variable changes. In this equation, the growth factor is 2.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.8.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q9-->
  <exercise data-content-id="8b18c59d-a4c8-4193-b9a3-f29c1675d9f2" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="\( t\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\( t\\)&quot;, &quot;\\( f(t)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> What is the independent variable?
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(t\), The independent variable is the variable that is manipulated or controlled and is represented by
        \(t\) in this equation.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.8.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q10-->
  <exercise data-content-id="0e063d30-b916-44a4-b667-c051bafdbcb0" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="\( f(t)\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\( t \\)&quot;, &quot;\\( f(t)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> What is the dependent variable?
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(f(t)\), The dependent variable is the variable that depends on or is influenced by the independent variable. In this
        equation, the dependent variable is \(f(t)\), which represents the output or value of the function.</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.8.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q11-->
  <exercise data-content-id="cb878148-c8cb-464b-9a7d-cfe8d9def838" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="\(y=25 \cdot 2^x\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(y=25 \\cdot (2x)^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=25 \\cdot 2^x\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=25 \\cdot x^2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(y=50 \\cdot 2^{\\frac 1x}\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which equation is most appropriate for modeling this data?</para>
      <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable">
        <thead/>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">x</entry>
            <entry>1</entry>
            <entry>2</entry>
            <entry>3</entry>
            <entry>4</entry>
            <entry>5</entry>
            <entry>6</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry scope="row" class="emphasis">y</entry>
            <entry>50</entry>
            <entry>100</entry>
            <entry>200</entry>
            <entry>400</entry>
            <entry>800</entry>
            <entry>1600</entry>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \(y=25 \cdot 2^x\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.8.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution> </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response"> </div>
</div>
  </section>
</content>
